**assistant**:
> Yes. The biggest issue is that the current design is doing far more allocation than the equal-resolution case needs, and there is at least one likely catastrophic copy/cache bug.
> 
> ### 1. Fix the pass-by-value river cache first
> 
> This is probably the main memory/time killer:
> 
> ```cpp
> HydroSurfaceNode* generateSurfaceNode(...,
>                                       map<unsigned, HydroNode*> rivers,
>                                       ...)
> ```
> 
> `rivers` is passed **by value**, so every surface cell gets a copied river map, and any river nodes generated are discarded from the caller’s cache. That means downstream river networks are regenerated many times.
> 
> Change to:
> 
> ```cpp
> HydroSurfaceNode* generateSurfaceNode(...,
>                                       map<unsigned, HydroNode*>& rivers,
>                                       ...)
> ```
> 
> Also change these to references:
> 
> ```cpp
> vector<HydroOutputNode*> outputNodes
> ```
> 
> to:
> 
> ```cpp
> const vector<HydroOutputNode*>& outputNodes
> ```
> 
> in `generateSurfaceNode`, `generateRiver`, and `followRiver`.
> 
> This alone may turn an impossible run into a merely large one.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 2. Do not generate surface nodes for ocean cells
> 
> Right now `generate()` loops over every ERA5/global cell and stores a `HydroSurfaceNode*` regardless of land/ocean. For oceans, `paths == 0`, causing division by zero here:
> 
> ```cpp
> max(total_dist / paths, min_dist / 4)
> total_slope / weight_slope
> ```
> 
> Add a mask check:
> 
> ```cpp
> if (mask_coarse.getDouble(rr, cc) < 0.5)
>     continue;
> ```
> 
> or store `outside`/`nullptr` explicitly.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 3. For 0.1° weather + 0.1° topography, avoid river subgraphs entirely
> 
> If coarse and fine grids are the same, the current abstraction is overkill. You do not need:
> 
> - surface-node aggregation,
> - recursive river generation,
> - `min_dist` expansion,
> - pointer graph construction,
> - `std::list` edges,
> - `std::map<unsigned, HydroNode*> rivers`.
> 
> Instead, build one node per land grid cell:
> 
> ```cpp
> struct SimpleHydroNode {
>     float area;
>     float slope;
>     float dist;
>     int downstream0;
>     int downstream1;
>     float weight0;
> };
> ```
> 
> For each land cell:
> 
> 1. Use `DInfinityMap::getDirections`.
> 2. Convert downstream lat/lon to grid indices.
> 3. Store one or two downstream integer IDs.
> 4. If downstream is ocean/outside, point to an output/sink ID.
> 
> This will be vastly smaller than millions of heap-allocated polymorphic objects with `std::list` and recursive serialization.
> 
> At global 0.1°, you have roughly:
> 
> \[
> 1800 \times 3600 \approx 6.5\text{ million cells}
> \]
> 
> A compact array representation is reasonable. A pointer/list/map graph with virtual objects can easily become many GB.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 4. Replace `std::map` where possible
> 
> For global raster IDs, `std::map<unsigned, HydroNode*>` is very expensive. Prefer:
> 
> ```cpp
> std::vector<HydroNode*> rivers(mask_fine.size(), nullptr);
> ```
> 
> or, better, integer node IDs:
> 
> ```cpp
> std::vector<int> downstream0;
> std::vector<int> downstream1;
> std::vector<float> weight0;
> ```
> 
> `std::map` costs multiple pointers plus allocator overhead per entry.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 5. Own/delete all generated nodes
> 
> Currently `created` only tracks copy-created nodes. Nodes made in `generate()` are leaked by `HydroNet::~HydroNet()` unless reachable elsewhere and manually deleted. Add ownership:
> 
> ```cpp
> created.push_back(surface);
> created.push_back(river);
> created.push_back(outside);
> created.push_back(ignore);
> ```
> 
> Better: use `std::unique_ptr<HydroNode>` and store raw pointers only as non-owning graph references.
> 
> This does not reduce peak memory during generation, but it prevents repeated-run and destructor leaks.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### 6. Avoid duplicate rasters
> 
> You load slope as `float`, then copy to `double`:
> 
> ```cpp
> GeographicMap<double>* slope_fine = new GeographicMap<double>(slope);
> ```
> 
> For global rasters, this matters. Keep slope as `float` if possible, or make `generate()` templated / accept `GeographicMap<float>&`.
> 
> Also watch this pattern:
> 
> ```cpp
> GeographicMap<bool>& mask_fine = *mask_lsm >= 0.5;
> ```
> 
> If `operator>=` returns a temporary or heap object, this is either dangerous or leaky. Make ownership explicit.
> 
> ---
> 
> ### My strongest recommendation
> 
> For the equal-resolution global case, create a separate `RasterHydroNet` / `CompactHydroNet` using integer node IDs and flat arrays. Keep the existing `HydroNet` for the low-res-weather / high-res-topography aggregation problem. Trying to force the pointer-heavy aggregated network to represent every 0.1° cell globally will remain fragile and memory-hungry even after bug fixes.
